requirements.requirement:
Create a versioned shared analysis layer that computes:
- planned versus completed work; comparable set/rep/load changes; correctly defined PRs and estimated max trends;
- relevant recent sessions for the same exercise/variant and goal;
- attendance relative to the actual schedule, distinguishing rest, unscheduled, missed, and partial days;
- session/week context, equipment constraints, the current program's progression result, and approved next-action candidates;
- missing/incomparable data and reasons a conclusion cannot be drawn.

Handle pounds/kilograms, per-hand/per-side loads, assisted/weighted bodyweight work, exercise variants, warmup versus work sets, unilateral totals, machine differences, and timed/distance activities. Do not aggregate tonnage across unlike modalities or treat tonnage as a universal progress score. Do not describe an estimated max as a measured max.

Use transparent, evidence-linked deterministic rules for progression/plateaus. A single poor session or a missing effort rating must not automatically produce a plateau diagnosis, fatigue diagnosis, or deload. Avoid unsupported causal statements about sleep, nutrition, injury, hormones, or motivation.

Build the packet from authenticated user-owned data. Never trust a client-supplied owner ID, an arbitrary workout ID, or a hash as authorization. For offline-first records that the server does not otherwise hold in plaintext, validate a minimized user-owned snapshot and recompute permitted metrics using the shared code. Document that workout logs are user-reported; authentication does not prove an athletic performance occurred. Do not require uploading the entire encrypted history or changing the privacy architecture just to calculate a summary.
